Tungsten carbide-cobalt (WC–Co) coatings are commonly used for industrial applications due to their high hardness, stiffness, and excellent wear resistance. While, the tungsten carbide (WC) is responsible for increasing the hardness, cobalt (Co) plays a pivotal role in improving adhesion and enhancing the ductility of the WC–Co mixture. Addition of 8–20% of binding elements (metals) such as cobalt and nickel (Ni) to carbide coatings is a practical way to increase their deposition rate. Various thermal spraying methods can be used for the deposition of WC–Co coatings such as high-velocity oxygen fuel (HVOF), high-velocity air fuel (HVAF), plasma spraying, and cold spraying (CS) [ 9 ,  10 ]. However, recent studies have explored the utilization of additive manufacturing (AM) for the deposition of WC-Co coatings, with a focus on laser-directed energy deposition (LDED) [ 11 ]. LDED holds several advantages over the conventional methods of deposition of WC–Co, such as a higher degree of control over spatial resolution, local control over composition and microstructure in multiple dimensions, and absence of deposition interface that provides a smooth transition from the substrate to the coating [ 12 ,  13 – 14 ]. Due to the excellent capabilities of LDED technology, it is speculated that utilization of this technique could improve deposition precision, better control of heat input that may result in improved microstructural uniformity, and enhanced mechanical performance compared to the ones deposited by thermal spray processes. Comparative analysis of LDED-processed and HVOF-processed WC–17Co samples confirmed formation of eutectic carbide phases (e.g., W  C 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
