A comparative analysis of the microstructure and mechanical properties of functionally graded WC-17Co with non-graded coatings produced via LDED was performed to evaluate the influence of deposition arrangement and texture on material’s integrity. Significant alterations in chemical composition and microstructure were observed in both LDED samples, including eutectic WC formation in binder regions and element diffusion from the substrate into interfacial layers. The same effects were previously observed for non-FGM WC-17Co samples produced by LDED. The differences in microstructure, phase, and composition characteristics between the FGM and non-FGM layered deposition of WC-17Co and steel fabricated via LDED were investigated, and the results are summarized below: XRD analysis showed that FGM samples had smoother phase transitions and reduced residual stresses, whereas non-FGM samples exhibited sharp phase boundaries that may cause more stress concentration. SEM analysis indicated that the FGM specimen exhibited gradual grain transition, enhancing metallurgical bonding, while the non-FGM sample had distinct layers with voids and porosity due to thermal expansion mismatch. EDS confirmed uniform and gradual elemental gradients in the FGM sample, whereas the non-FGM sample showed abrupt transitions. Grain size measurement showed acceptable agreement between the results obtained from microstructural observation, XRD, and EBSD experiments for WC phases. For the non-FGM sample, the calculated WC lattice parameters fell within the range reported in the literature, while the FGM sample showed substantially greater deviation. This could be associated with the higher content of Co in the FGM sample (region 3), and a larger coefficient of thermal expansion of Co binder compared to WC. Due to high temperatures in the LDED process, Co phase surrounding the WC particles develops compressive residual stress which will lead to lower lattice parameters in WC. The lattice parameters o